      "quotedTweet": {
        "id": "2044495914467803362",
        "text": "its so insane to think about how we paid drip thousands of dollars a month at our last start up to power email stuff\n\ni built my own email marketing studio paltform tailored to what i need and costs $15 a month with @postmarkapp lol\n\nhow we got here:\n>started with manual emails via gmail and pulling user emails from db manually with a 'reply stop' line ;) \n>manually pulled email lists every week and manually removed users from email list who replied stop \n>no email tracking but obvious evidence that newsletters boosted user engagement - enough signal to keep sending newsletters\n>user email count got too big, started getting blocked by gmail\n>built a a newsletter composer app with lovable, connected to my prod db and powered by postmark\n>now newsletters are fully automated except writing the content. oh and we got tracking in postmark too (nice!) but had to log into to postmark to see stuff\n>social media marketing is time expensive, what else can we do to boost engagement and sales? newsletters work decent, so lets try targeted email campaigns!\n>extended my simple newsletter composer app with @Lovable into a full fledged email marketing platform\n>i used lovable and @claudeai  code and @supabase edge functions to set up all of my triggers and the guard rails and run simulations with testing features using live prod db data to understand what would happen if we went live and iterated from there\n>went live yesterday and its running perfectly! we currently have 3 targeted email campaigns running autonomously and dashboard pulls in the postmark analytics into our dashboard for easy viewing\n>going to add a few more this week and see what happens\n\nthe jump from simple newsletter composter to full automated targeted email platform with triggers/guardrails/analytics etc took me a couple full days of work and not spending hundreds on some bloated crm/marketing tool like drip (sorry drip loved you but now useless)",
        "author": {
          "screenName": "randyLunetto",
          "name": "randy lunetto"
        }
      }
    },
